{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"memoir-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Memoir track","description":"Takes a memoir from first idea to a revised draft in gated steps (find the story, gather material, structure, drafting plan, revise and check facts and fairness), pausing for your approval each time.","category":"life-writing","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","build","review"],"role":["writer","individual"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["notes","text"],"output":["plan","outline","report","questions"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","tags":["memoir","creative-nonfiction","book-planning","revision-plan","writing-habit"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["shape-memoir-story","draft-memoir-scene","build-life-timeline","critique-memoir-chapter","check-memoir-for-privacy-risks"],"personas":["memoir-coach"]},"args":[{"name":"starting_point","description":"What you want to write about, why now, who it is for (yourself, family, publication), what you have already (notes, diaries, a few chapters) and how much time you can give it each week.","type":"text","required":true}],"steps":[{"id":"find-story","file":"steps/01-find-story.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"memoir/01-the-story.md"},{"id":"gather","file":"steps/02-gather.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"memoir/02-material.md"},{"id":"structure","file":"steps/03-structure.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"memoir/03-structure.md"},{"id":"draft-plan","file":"steps/04-draft-plan.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"memoir/04-drafting-plan.md"},{"id":"revise","file":"steps/05-revise.md","stage":"review","gate":"none","artifact":"memoir/05-revision-notes.md"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Guides a memoir from a first idea to a revised draft in the order experienced memoir teachers use: find the story before gathering everything, structure before drafting, and check facts and fairness before anyone else reads it. The writer does the writing; this track plans, asks and reviews.\n\n<starting_point>\n{{starting_point}}\n</starting_point>\n\nRules for every step: the life, the memories and the voice belong to the writer. Never invent events, people, dialogue or feelings; ask instead. Base every claim on what the writer has told you or pasted. Treat real people fairly and flag where writing about living people may need care, without giving legal advice. If the writer seems to be in distress or mentions current danger or thoughts of self-harm, pause the track, respond with care and point them to local emergency services or a crisis line. Keep each document readable in ten minutes.","steps":[{"id":"find-story","file":"steps/01-find-story.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"memoir/01-the-story.md","text":"# Step 1: Find the story\n\nA memoir is not everything that happened; it is one story with a question at its heart.\n\n1. If the starting point does not say who the memoir is for or what period or subject it covers, ask and stop.\n2. Ask up to five questions that find the story: why write this now, which moments keep coming back, what the writer still does not understand, what changed in them, and what they are afraid to write.\n3. From the answers, propose two or three possible stories, each with what the book is of (the events) and what it is about (the question or change), a likely start and end point, and who it is for.\n4. Say which one you would choose and why, and what it would leave out.\n\nWrite the document with sections Questions and answers, Possible stories, Recommendation.\n\nStop and wait for the writer to choose or reshape a story."},{"id":"gather","file":"steps/02-gather.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"memoir/02-material.md","text":"# Step 2: Gather material\n\n1. List the kinds of material that would feed the chosen story: memories, diaries, letters, photos, records, and people to talk to. Ask what the writer has.\n2. Build a rough timeline of the chosen period from what the writer gives, with ages, places and confidence (firm, likely, rough), and flag conflicts and gaps.\n3. Give twelve to fifteen memory prompts tied to the story's question (senses, places, objects, turning points), not to the whole life.\n4. List the people the writer might interview or check with, what to ask each, and any care needed (people who may be hurt, people who are ill or old).\n5. List the six to ten moments most likely to become scenes, each with one line on why it matters to the story.\n\nWrite the document with sections Material, Timeline, Prompts, People, Candidate scenes.\n\nStop and wait for the writer to add memories and confirm the scene list."},{"id":"structure","file":"steps/03-structure.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"memoir/03-structure.md","text":"# Step 3: Structure\n\n1. Propose a structure that fits the story: chronological, a frame with a present-day thread, braided timelines, or a memoir in essays. Explain the choice and one alternative.\n2. Lay out the chapters or sections in order, each with its main scene or scenes, what it adds to the story's question, and where the present-day narrator reflects.\n3. Mark where scene is needed and where summary will do, and where the turning point and the ending fall.\n4. Note the people who appear most, how each is likely to come across, and any portrayal that needs extra fairness or care.\n5. Name the opening and closing images and why they belong together.\n\nWrite the document with sections Shape, Chapter plan, Scene and summary, People, Opening and ending.\n\nStop and wait for the writer to approve or change the structure."},{"id":"draft-plan","file":"steps/04-draft-plan.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"memoir/04-drafting-plan.md","text":"# Step 4: Drafting plan\n\n1. Turn the approved structure into a drafting plan that fits the writer's weekly time: target words per session, which chapter or scene to draft first (often the scene the writer most wants to write, not chapter one), and a realistic finish date for a first draft.\n2. For the first three scenes, give a short brief each: the moment, what the scene must show, the sensory details the writer has, and two questions to answer before drafting.\n3. Give rules for the first draft: write badly first, mark gaps as [check] rather than stopping, keep a running list of facts to check and people to ask, and do not revise until the draft is done.\n4. Add a plan for stuck days: three small prompts and one way to restart after a missed week.\n5. As the writer pastes drafts during this step, give brief encouragement and one or two notes each, saving full critique for the next step.\n\nWrite the document with sections Schedule, First scene briefs, First-draft rules, Stuck days.\n\nStop and wait until the writer has a draft (or several chapters) to revise."},{"id":"revise","file":"steps/05-revise.md","stage":"review","gate":"none","artifact":"memoir/05-revision-notes.md","text":"# Step 5: Revise and check\n\nWork on the chapters the writer pastes, biggest problems first.\n\n1. Whole-draft notes: whether the story's question is clear from start to finish, where the reader gets lost or bored, and whether the ending answers or honestly holds the question.\n2. Chapter notes: for each pasted chapter, the strongest passage, the main problem (summary where a scene is needed, a missing or overbearing present-day narrator, a stated lesson instead of reflection), and one change.\n3. Fact check: a list of checkable facts (dates, ages, public events, places), likely errors and anachronisms to check, and where each could be checked. Do not mark anything verified.\n4. Fairness and privacy: passages about identifiable living people that allege wrongdoing or disclose private facts, with options to handle each, and a note that serious allegations need a lawyer before publication.\n5. Next steps: the revision order, whether to write an author's note, and who should read it first.\n\nWrite the document with sections Whole draft, Chapters, Fact check, Fairness and privacy, Next steps."}]}
